  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nWeak and mainly dry cold front will continue to push across the\nstate tonight. Some breezy northwest winds will be found as a\nresult, along with low afternoon and early evening humidity\nvalues. This could bring some fire weather concerns, although\nwinds are overall forecast to remain below near critical fire\nweather thresholds. Surface high then briefing settles in\ntonight, bringing continued clear skies along with light and\nvariable winds. Look for lows tonight in the 30s as a result. \nSome areas could see lows near to perhaps slightly below \nfreezing. Most of these areas in the north and central where \nfreeze conditions have likely already occurred. With forecast \nnear freezing tonight, and some uncertainty on mid to high \nclouds, will hold off on any Freeze Highlights for now. \nConditions should remain too dry for fog development despite \ncalming conditions tonight. Surface high moves eastward Monday, \nwith weak ridging aloft. The surface gradient will bring a \nsoutherly wind Monday, with some breezy conditions across the \nwest. These winds combined with very dry conditions should mix \nout lower dewpoints than NBM forecast, thus lowered these \nslightly. This lowering of dewpoints brings afternoon RH in the \n20s across the west. Combine this with sustained winds near or \nslightly higher than 20 mph could bring some isolated near \ncritical fire weather conditions across western North Dakota \nMonday afternoon and early evening. Otherwise look for slightly \ncooler temperatures on Monday, although highs will still remain \nin the upper 50s to upper 60s. \n\nMild and dry conditions then look to continue Tuesday through\nThursday as upper level ridging continues across the area. Highs\nin the 60s will be found during this time period, with slightly\nwarmer temperatures in the 70s possible on Thursday as southwest\nflow aloft is forecast to return to the area. This pattern could\nalso bring some breezy winds on Thursday. Mild lows at night \nare also expected, with mainly above freezing temperatures \nforecast. Later in the week into the upcoming weekend will start\nwith above normal temperatures and breezy conditions. Clusters \nstill indicate trough pattern to push across the region later in\nthe week. There still remains some split flow concerns with \nthis system, although this remains the next chance for \nprecipitation. Temperatures still look to remain warm enough for\nrain with any precipitation that may fall. Even though \nprecipitation is moderate confidence at this point, there is \nsome higher confidence in cooler, perhaps more near normal, \ntemperatures moving in this disturbance later this week. Breezy \nwinds may also continue to at least start the weekend as this \npotential system moves through.",
